		 ___________________________________________
		|					    |
		|    WELCOME TO VEDIT PLUS VERSION 3.71     |
		|___________________________________________|

This file is primarily oriented towards previous VEDIT PLUS users. It gives
an overview of the changes made from version 3.0 to 3.71.



		CHANGES FROM 3.70 TO 3.71
		-------------------------

Monochrome	VEDIT now has two sets of configurable screen attributes; one
Attributes	for Color displays and one for Monochrome displays. Also
		useful for Laptop computers.

Mouse		VEDIT's mouse support is optimized for the Microsoft and
Compatibility	Logitech drivers. To support Mouse Systems (and perhaps
Options		other) drivers, a compatibility option(s) must be selected
		with {CONFIG, Misc, Mouse options}.

Max [REPEAT]	The maximum [REPEAT] count of 256 can now be configured with
count		"Max [REPEAT] count" in the VEDIT.CFG file or with the "YJO"
		command from Command Mode. Note: VEDIT may not be able to
		undo the effects of very large repeat counts!

WordStar	The revised WORDSTAR.KEY file improves VEDIT's WordStar
Emulation	emulation.

.KEY file	Keyboard layout files, e.g. VEDIT.KEY, now treat lines
Comments	beginning with "R*" as comment lines. (Note: only applicable
		to keyboard layout files saved in "text" format.)

On-line		The on-line calculator at the "COMMAND:" prompt can accept
calculator	hexadecimal numbers and optionally display the result in
		hex. See ERRATA.DOC.

Overstrike	VEDIT can now operate in overstrike mode inside dialog boxes
mode		and at the "COMMAND:" prompt. Pressing [INSERT] toggles
		between insert and overstrike mode. It is independent of the
		Visual Mode's "Insert mode".

Tab Stops	Uniform Tab stops, e.g. every 4 or 8, now apply for any line
		length. (Previously limited to column 254 or 33 tab stops.)

{MISC, VGA/EGA	This menu item now toggles a VGA through 25, 28 and 50 line
toggle}		modes. VEDIT fully supports the VGA 28-line mode.


Technical Changes:
------------------

RB command	The new command "RB(r)/string/" compares text register 'r'
		to the string 'string'. This is a very useful command.

		The following macro example prompts the user for a string and
		tests whether the user entered "red", "white" or "blue".

		[
		:RQ(9)"Enter desired color: "
		RB(9)"red" (.rv=0)[ YT"Handle Red case" JL ]
		RB(9)"white" (.rv=0)[ YT"Handle White case" JL ]
		RB(9)"blue" (.rv=0)[ YT"Handle Blue case" JL ]
		YA YEN YT/Please enter "red", "white" or "blue"./ YEN
		]

JP command	The "JP/label/" command now works in keystroke macros.
		'label' may no longer include pattern matching codes,
		however "|Rr", e.g. "JP/|R9/" is still supported and very
		useful.

XT command	The new "\" option on "\XT(x)" displays numeric register 'x'
		in hexadecimal.

OD/OT commands	The format of the date displayed by "OD" was changed. For
		example, the previous "3-09-93" is now "03-09-93". The "OT"
		command now displays "9:08:52" as " 9:08:52", i.e with a
		leading space.



		CHANGES FROM 3.61 TO 3.70 (Some were added in 3.62)
		-------------------------

****************************************************************************
NOTE:	Several changes were made to the "normal" keyboard in order to make
	it more Microsoft and Borland compatible:

	[ENTER CTRL] was changed from <Ctrl-Q> to <Ctrl-P>.

	{PRINT,Block} was changed from <Ctrl-P> to <Ctrl-O>.

	{MISC,Keyboard layout,Add macro} was changed from <Ctrl-K> to <Alt-A>.

NOTE:	The extremely rarely used pattern matching code "|G" (for
	parentheses matching) has been changed to "|P".

NOTE:	Any existing command macros using the rarely used "YC" command will
	need modification.  See ERRATA.DOC.
****************************************************************************

Data/binary	The record size for binary files in {CONFIG, File handling,
file support	File type} can now be set to any value between 8 and 2048.

		The "horizontal scroll margin" can now be set to any value
		between 40 and 2048. This can help in editing lines longer
		than 2048 in "text" mode.

Mac text	Mac style text files that use a Carriage-Return, instead of
files		a Line-Feed as the "newline" character are now supported.
		VEDIT will normally auto-detect these files and set {CONFIG,
		File handling, File type} to the new value of "2".

Compare		The new {SEARCH, Compare buffers} performs a quick comparison
buffers		of two buffers (files) from their current cursor positions.

Goto file	The new {GOTO, File position} permits a quick "jump" to any
position	absolute file position. The position can be entered in hex.

Hexadecimal	Any number can now be entered in hexadecimal by preceding it
numbers		with "0h". For example, "0h29af" is identical to "10671".
		Mixed radix expressions such as "0hffff-2000" are also valid.

		The command mode can perform a hex to decimal conversion.
		Simply enter the hex number at the "COMMAND:" prompt and it
		will return the decimal equivalent.

Point and	The point and shoot displays filenames in alphabetic order and
Shoot		supports incremental searching.

Much faster	Having a mouse driver installed no longer slows down startup
startup		very much. (VEDIT now only resets the mouse driver on startup
		instead of also the mouse hardware.)

Unlimited	An unlimited number of screen lines are now supported. The
screen lines	previous limit was 60.

[BACKSPACE]	The new {CONFIG, Emulation, [BACKSPACE] emulation mode}
emulation mode	optionally permits [BACKSPACE] to only be destructive in
		"Insert" mode. In "Overstrike" mode it only moves the cursor
		left. [BACKSPACE] can also optionally stop at the beginning of
		line.

Pattern		The new pattern matching code "|Hhh" matches the character
Matching	with hexadecimal value 'hh'. Convenient during hex-mode
		editing. Similarly, "|ddd" matches the character with decimal
		value 'ddd'. The previously used "|0" must be changed to
		"|000".

		The code "|G" matches graphics (high-bit) characters.

Improved block	[T-REG COPY/MOVE] and [BLOCK COPY/MOVE] no longer require the
operations	block to be in memory. The "FILE POSITION NOT IN MEMORY" error
		no longer exists. However, the new error "BLOCK IS TOO LARGE"
		is still possible.

		<Tab> characters no longer confuse columnar block
		highlighting. [CURSOR UP/DOWN] can now also move through the
		middle of a <Tab>.

{EDIT, Lower/	Columnar blocks are now also supported. Any block operation
 upper case}	now cancels the block.

Technical Changes:
------------------

Columnar block	Command macros using columnar block commands such as "@RC"
commands	previously worked erratically if executed as keystroke
		macros or from {MISC, Execute macro}. This has been fixed.

G command	":G" command option moves the block markers to the new block.
		"p,qG" no longer requires 'p' and 'q' to be in memory.

YN command	"nYN" inserts 'n' "newlines" according to the current file
		type. Macros using "13EI 10EI" should be changed to "YN"
		to handle non-DOS file types.

YYG command	The new "YYG" command is similar to the "G" command, but
		also deletes the original block; similar to [BLOCK MOVE].

D, K, T, PR,	In "p,q:D", "p,q:K", "p,q:T", "p,q:PR", "p,q:RC", "p,q:YB" and
RC, YB, YS	"p,q:YS", the ":" command option suppresses movement of the
commands	edit position.

YC command	The "YC" command and new "YCB" and "YCE" commands now set
		block begin/end columns that correspond to those set with
		{BLOCK, Set column marker}. See ERRATA.DOC.

	NOTE:	Any existing macros using "YC" must be modified!

XV command	The new "XV(x)" command reads the numeric expression at the
		edit position and sets numeric register 'x'.

EB command	The new option ":EB" opens the file in "browse mode".

New Internal	.cc - Column position of the cursor; same as "COL:" display
values		.cb - Block-begin marker's corresponding column
		.ce - Block-end marker's corresponding column
		.kt - Total number of keys pressed since VEDIT was started

		The previous internal values ".cl" and ".cr" have been
		replaced with ".cb" and ".ce"



		CHANGES FROM 3.60 TO 3.61
		-------------------------

Multiple file	You can startup VEDIT with up to 26 files. The wildcards "*"
startup		and "?" can also be used. E.g. "VEDIT *.C *.H".

Auto-save	VEDIT can be configured to auto-save all modified files after
		a configurable number of minutes.

Highlight	VEDIT can be configured to highlight the entire line that the
cursor line	cursor is on. Either "bright" characters or an overriding
		color can be used.

Improved file	The new {FILE, Buffer toggle}, assigned to <Alt-T> toggles the
and window	current window between all files being edited. (This is a
handling	little different from the previous <Alt-T> which toggled
		between buffers and windows.)

		The new {WINDOW, Window toggle}, assigned to <Ctrl-T> toggles
		between windows.

Binary file	Binary files that have no LF (line-feed) or excessively long
support		lines are handled as simply 16 or 64 characters per line.
		This is controlled with {CONFIG, File handling, File type}
		and {CONFIG, File handling, Auto-file type}.

{FILE, Exit}	{FILE, Quit} has been deleted. {FILE, Exit} has a new option
{FILE, Quit}	"[Q]uit-all" to quit all files.

		In {FILE, Exit}, the previous option "[A]ll-files" has been
		renamed to "[S]ave-all".

POS: yyyy:xxxx	When editing in hexadecimal, the cursor's character offset
		into the file is displayed on the status line.

EBCDIC display	{WINDOW, Toggle display mode} also toggles into EBCDIC mode.

WILDFILE.VDM	The WILDFILE macro has been improved so that it can operate
		on files currently being edited. This is the best way of
		performing search/replace on multiple files. You can run
		WILDFILE by selecting it from the {MISC} menu.

SORT.VDM	The SORT macro is faster and can realistically handle files
		up to about 1 Megabyte.

CFUNC.VDM	The new CFUNC macro lists each C program routine declaration
		in a separate window; as you move through the list, the
		original window moves through the C program.

COLOR.VDM	The new COLOR macro lets you select among common color
		schemes for the text and menus. (It is used during
		installation.)



		CHANGES FROM 3.51 TO 3.60 (Some were added in 3.52 and 3.53)
		-------------------------

Menu operation	The operation of the menu system has been improved and now
		operates identically to that on recent Microsoft and Borland
		products which follow the IBM "SAA/CUA" standard.

		VEDIT normally lets you "preview" sub-menus before they are
		selected. Since this is non-standard (but we think very
		desirable), the configuration option {CONFIG, Screen display,
		Enable sub-menu preview} lets you control this feature. By
		default it is set to "Yes". (However, on the Demo version, it
		defaults to "No".)

Menu items	The order of items in the main menu has been changed according
		to the "CUA" standard. "Undo" is now a sub-menu in the "Edit"
		menu. "Insert file" has been moved to the "Edit" menu.

{BLOCK} menu	The {BLOCK} menu now always displays all items. The inactive
		items are displayed in a different color. The color is
		controlled with "Color override for inactive menu items".

New {FILE}	{FILE, New} now opens a new, empty edit buffer. {FILE, Open}
menu items	now lets you select whether the file is opened in the same
		edit buffer or in a new buffer. {FILE, Close} closes the
		current edit buffer, letting you save or abandon the file.
		{FILE, Save all} saves all modified files. The old {FILE,
		Read-only} has been changed to {FILE, Browse mode}.

	Note:	The <Alt-N> keystroke macro which previously was {FILE, New}
		is now {FILE, Open}-S.

{FILE, Exit}	"Exit" now displays each modified buffer (file) and prompts
		whether it is to be saved or abandoned. It repeats this for
		each buffer and then exits VEDIT. If a buffer to be saved has
		no assigned filename, you are prompted for one. At the {FILE,
		Exit} prompt, you can reply with "[S]ave-all" to quickly save
		all files which have assigned filenames.

	Note:	To exit just the current buffer (file) and switch to another
		buffer you previously used {FILE, Exit}-Y. You should now use
		{FILE, Close}.

Fast browse	You can quickly browse files by invoking VEDIT with the "-b"
mode		option. You can also open a file in browse-only mode by
		selecting {FILE, New}, {FILE, Browse mode} and then {FILE,
		Open}-S. {GOTO, Beginning/End of file} and relative jumps
		using the mouse are instantaneous. CD-ROM and other read-only
		files are automatically opened in browse-only mode.

Filename	The filename on the status line is preceded with "!" when the
		file was opened in browse-only mode. The file cannot be
		altered.

{Help, Edit	Files which have been altered since they were last saved are
buffers}	preceded with a "*".

"-a filename"	You can edit an existing file and save it as another name by
option		using the "-a filename" option. This is a convenient shortcut
		for opening the file and then selecting another name with
		{FILE, Save as}. This option can be used when you invoke
		VEDIT:

			vedit prototyp.let -a bobsmith.let

		This option can also be used at the filename prompt for
		{FILE, Open}.

Cursor Type	{CONFIG, Misc} now has separate cursor types for overstrike
		and insert modes.

		The "System" cursor has been expanded to three types:
		4 - Thin cursor
		5 - Medium cursor
		6 - Full height cursor

[REPEAT]	The "repeat" count is now followed with <Enter>. It then
		prompts for the key to repeat.

[DEL NEXT WORD]	These functions now first delete the characters in a word, and
[DEL PREV WORD]	when pressed again, the characters between words.

Color		Screen colors can now be configured with the {CONFIG, Colors}
Configuration	sub-menu.

WILDFILE.VDM	The macro has been enhanced and made more robust. It can now
		process files currently being edited.

Keyboard	The "normal" keyboard has been changed; it is similar to the
layout		"alternate" keyboard layout in 3.5x. The new "alternate"
		keyboard layout is almost identical to the 3.5x "normal"
		layout. See "Keyboard Layout" in Chapter 2 for details.

	<Ctrl-Y>  Is [ERASE LINE]; you can still use <Ctrl-X>.
	<F1>	  Is now [HELP].
	<F10>	  Is now [MENU]

		Previous F10 operations are now on F11 and the "Grey" keys:

	<Grey+> and <Ctrl-F11> are now [T-REG COPY]
	<Grey-> and <Alt-F11> are now [T-REG MOVE]
	<Grey*> and <F11> are now [T-REG INSERT].

Technical Changes:
------------------

EA command	The command "EAA" saves all modified files and lets you
		continue editing. It is identical to {FILE, Save all}.

EB command	The "EB" command supports the new "-a filename" option.
		Use the on-line help for more information.

EQ command	The command "EQX" prompts for verification that you want to
		quit each modified buffer. It is identical to {FILE, Quit}.

EX command	The command "EXX" prompts whether each modified buffer is
		to be changed. It is identical to {FILE, Exit}.

YQ command	The command "YQ filename" tests whether 'filename' is being
		edited in any edit buffer and sets ".rv" accordingly.

New Internal	.ba - Number of altered edit buffers
values		.bf - Name of next free edit buffer in range "A"-"Z"
		.bm - Browse mode flag (0=No, 1=Yes)
		.bt - Total number of edit buffers
		.fs - File size of the file being edited
		.ic - DELETED - Use "YJU 1" instead
		.ob - File opened in browse-only mode (0=No, n=modes)
		.sv - IBM PC video mode
		.wb - Type of window borders
		.wd - Window display mode (see "Screen display options")
		.wr - Reverse video of current window attribute



		CHANGES FROM 3.50 TO 3.51
		-------------------------

Scroll bars	Editing windows can optionally be displayed with scroll bars.
		The scroll bars work according to the "CUA" standard.

Window borders	With scroll bars enabled, all windows normally have full
		borders. You can also select full borders without scroll bars,
		the default minimal borders or no borders.

Mouse support	Enhanced mouse support works with the optional scroll bars.
		Block highlighting is simplified and improved.

		When scroll bars are not displayed, the right button can
		access "stealth" scroll bars at the right and bottom edges
		of the window.

		The mouse can be used to make selection in VEDIT's simple
		dialog boxes, e.g. "[Y]es [N]o" type prompts.

		The mouse can make selections within the compiler support
		macro.

2 Gigabyte	The internal math has been expanded from 24 bit to 32 bit
file size	precision, increasing VEDIT's maximum file size to
		2 Gigabytes.

		{HELP, Status} now displays the current file size. This
		size includes any current edit changes.

Hex-mode	The new function {WINDOW, Hex-mode split} lets you edit a file
editing		in two windows; one displays and lets you edit in hexadecimal,
		the other in normal ASCII.

		The new function {WINDOW, Toggle display mode} toggles the
		current window between six display modes, including
		hexadecimal.

		{CONFIG, Screen Display, Screen display options} sets the
		default screen mode. (The settings have changed from previous
		versions!)

Block Delete	Pressing [DELETE] with the cursor within a highlighted block
		now deletes the entire block. {CONFIG, Emulation, Special
		emulation modes} can enable/disable this feature.

Regular		A new search option {SEARCH, Config, Maximize regular
Expressions	expression matching} determines whether regular expressions
		always match the longest possible string as described on pages
		98-99 of the 2.10 VEDIT User's manual.



		CHANGES FROM 3.40 TO 3.50
		-------------------------

[BACK TAB]	[BACK TAB] has been renamed to [LINE BEGIN]. [ZIP] has been
[ZIP]		renamed to [LINE END]. {CONFIG, Emulation, Line emulation
		mode} controls how these functions work.

[FIND]		Has been renamed to [SEARCH].

[FIND AGAIN]	Has been renamed to [SEARCH AGAIN].

[LINE TOGGLE]	Has been deleted. It can be simulated with the keystroke macro:

			[VISUAL EXIT] $EM"|>" .rv=0[ 0L ][ $N"|>" ]

[SCREEN TOGGLE]	Has been replaced with [SCREEN BEGIN] and [SCREEN END].
		It can be simulated with the keystroke macro:

			[VISUAL EXIT] (.wy<.wl/2)[ YV/\SE\/ ][ YV/\SB\/ ]

[TAB CURSOR]	Has been renamed to [NEXT TAB STOP].

[T-REG INSERT]	New function inserts the specified text register. [T-REG COPY]
		no longer inserts the text register when no markers are set.

[REPEAT LAST]	New function that repeats the last keystroke, keystroke macro
		or menu function.

[FORMAT		This Edit Function has been deleted. It is still available in
 PARAGRAPH]	the {EDIT} menu. It is still assigned to <CTRL-F>.

Keyboard	All changes to the keyboard layout are now made with the
Layout		{MISC, Keyboard layout} sub-menu. This includes changing the
		basic keyboard layout, adding Keystroke Macros, displaying and
		printing the layout.

		Keyboard layouts saved to disk as ".KEY" files can now be
		easily changed, edited and loaded back in. Many users will
		want to handle all keyboard layout changes in this way. See
		the supplied WORDSTAR.KEY and BRIEF.KEY files for examples.

		VEDIT now supports all enhanced keyboard keys including
		<CTRL-Cursor-keys>, <Shift-Cursor-keys>, <Shift-Ins>, etc.

		The "normal" keyboard layout for [PREV PARAGRAPH],
		[NEXT PARAGRAPH], [SCROLL UP], [SCROLL DOWN], {GOTO, Beginning
		of file}, {GOTO, End of file} and the block handling has been
		changed.

Keystroke	The [REPEAT] and new [REPEAT LAST] functions can now be used
Macros		with any keystroke macro.

Block		Many changes (improvements) were made to the overall block
Handling	handling and related keyboard layout.

		Columnar block operations are now selected with {BLOCK, Set
		column marker}.

		<F9> is now {BLOCK, Set marker}. It only sets the block
		markers and no longer copies a block. <F9> can now be used
		to change the size of an already highlighted block.

		<CTRL-F9> is now [BLOCK COPY]. <ALT-F9> is still [BLOCK MOVE].

		<Grey*> is the new function [T-REG INSERT]. It also inserts a
		Text register, even if you have already started to highlight a
		block.

		<Grey+> is now [T-REG COPY]. <Grey-> is now [T-REG MOVE].

		Previously, <F9> and <F10> were "smart" keys whose function
		depended upon how many block markers were set. This turned
		out to be confusing to many users.

		The following key assignments emulate the old 3.40 layout:

	<F9>	   [VISUAL EXIT] (.be=-1)[.pXBE][YV/\ME\BC/]
	<ALT-F9>   [VISUAL EXIT] (.be=-1)[.pXBE][YV/\ME\BM/]
	<F10>	   [VISUAL EXIT] (.bb=-1)[YV/\ME\BI/][YV/\ME\BP/]
	<ALT-F10>  [VISUAL EXIT] (.bb=-1)[YV/\ME\BI/][YV/\ME\BV/]

R/O Files	{FILE, Browse mode} prevents edit changes to this file. This
		mode is automatically selected and cannot be changed when
		editing a Read-only file. The "-B" invocation option also
		forces this mode for all files.

Change		"Change Directory" has been deleted from the {FILE} menu.
Directory	Instead, VEDIT now remembers the last directory selected with
		{FILE, Directory} via "point and shoot".

"Print" to	{PRINT, Config} lets you print to a file. Can either have
file		VEDIT prompt for the filename, or always print to the same
		file.

Mouse		With {CONFIG, Misc, Mouse cursor} set to "1", the mouse now
		starts in the upper left hand corner. Moving the mouse cursor
		to this corner also makes it invisible.

VCONFIG		The previous VPCONFIG program has been replaced with VCONFIG,
Program		which is a much smaller and simpler program. Most
		configuration changes that can now be made with the {CONFIG}
		menu or {MISC, Keyboard layout} sub-menu have been removed
		from VCONFIG.

Alternate	{CONFIG, Save to disk} saves ALL configuration parameters
Configuration	(except keyboard configuration) to a "VEDIT.CFG" file. This
		file can easily be edited and loaded into VEDIT with {CONFIG,
		Load from disk}. Examining this file helps understand the
		new "YJx" commands for changing configuration parameters.

Startup		VEDIT can be configured to always startup in a particular
screen size	screen size such as 25 or 43/50 line mode.

Screen restore	Upon exit, VEDIT can optionally restore the screen size and
options		also the original screen contents.

Invocation	New invocation options "-B", "-O" and "-nnn", and revised
Options		"-S" are described in the manual.

/?		Displays a summary of all invocation options.

Environment	The optional environment variable "VEDPATH" can be used to
Variables	override the configured "VEDIT Home Directory". For example:

			SET VEDPATH=D:\UTIL\VEDIT

		The optional variable "VEDIT" can be used to set any desired
		default invocation switches. For example:

			SET VEDIT=+M +S196

Compiler	The compiler support has been improved.
Support
		If you have supported any custom compilers by creating your
		own ".VCS" files, a simple change must be made to them at
		the "!FUNC-6!" label. See CL.VCS for the necessary change.



Technical Changes:
------------------

ES and EO	EO 8 (Insert Mode) has been changed to ES 13. EO 9 (Column
commands	Mode) has been changed to ES 14. EO 13 (Reset Undo stack)
		has been changed to an emulation function; it should normally
		be set to "0".

RN command	"RN(r) file" loads 'file' into register 'r' and chains (jumps)
		to it; 'r' can be the currently executing register.

RV command	RV(r)/name/ loads the value of environment variable 'name'
		into text register 'r'.

YJ..		New commands for changing, loading and saving configuration.
  YJS file	Save entire configuration to the file 'file'.
  YJL file	Load the configuration file 'file'.
  YJV VEDIT.EXE  Save the entire configuration into the VEDIT.EXE file.
 +YJV VEDIT.EXE  Also saves the keyboard layout into VEDIT.EXE.
  YJA		Display/change the configuration colors (attributes).
  YJB		Display/change the configuration window border characters.
  YJM		Display/change the memory mapped screen parameters.
  YJN		Display/change the configuration screen parameters.
  YJO		Display/change the configuration undo parameters.
  YJU		Display/change the configuration user parameters.

YK..		New commands for changing the keyboard layout from the
		macro language. Use help command "H YKA" for details.
  YKA		Add a key assignment to the keyboard layout table.
  YKD		Delete a key assignment from the keyboard layout table.
  YKI		Insert (temporary) a key assignment.
  YKP		Pop (remove) temporary key assignments.

Borderless	The "0" parameter in front of the YWB, YWT, YWR and YWL commands
Windows		creates borderless windows.

":" option	The ":" option for the commands ":F", ":S" and ":L" is now
		equivalent to setting "ES 8 2". The ":" suppresses the error
		message and only sets the ".er" and/or ".es" error flags; it
		no longer jumps out of the iteration loop. Use ".erJL" or the
		equivalent ";" to jump out of iteration loops when necessary.

Search		|>	Always matches the End-of-file.
Enhancements	|L	Never matches the End-of-file.

		Search/replace string can include "/" or other delimiter; use
		"|/" or "\/" to include the delimiter in a search string, e.g.
		"F/17+7|/4/" is a search for "17+7/4".

New Internal	.bol -   True if at beginning of line
values		.cm  -   True if columnar block markers are set
		.eol -   True if at end of line
		.im  -   True if Insert mode is on
		.ip  -   Index into pattern matching "pattern set"
		.kc  -   Wait for and return next keyboard character
		.kf(n) - Value of 'n'th previous keyboard char/function code
		.kp(n) - Value of 'n'th previous raw keystroke
		.ks  -   Return keyboard status
		.le  -   Number of chars at normal end-of-line (CR/LF=2, LF=1)
		.ma  -   True if mouse is active
		.mv  -   True when this is a visual mode macro
		.ol(x) - Flag whether invocation option 'x' was specified;
			 supports custom invocation options
		.or  -   Flag whether edit buffer is in Read-only mode
		.rc  -   Return code from last program run from within VEDIT
		.sm  -   True if screen only supports monochrome attributes
		.tt  -   Time ticks; increment at 18 per second
		.wq  -   Name of "next" window attached to an edit buffer



		ADDITIONAL CHANGES FROM 3.30
		----------------------------

V-SWAP		The V-SWAP programs permits VEDIT to completely swap itself
		and any desired TSRs out of memory when shelling out to DOS.
		This makes more memory available for running large compilers
		and debuggers.

Compiler	Compiler Support integrates compilers, assemblers, linkers,
Support		debuggers and Make utilities. The built-in keystroke macro
		<ALT-I> starts up the Compiler Support.

Backup Files	{CONFIG, File handling, Enable backup files} determines
		whether VEDIT creates ".BAK" files. In Command Mode this is ES
		12.

Printer		{PRINT, Config, Printer} lets you print to "PRN:" as before,
Support		or directly to "LPT1:", "LPT2:", "LPT3:", "COM1:", "COM2:" or
		"COM3:". {PRINT, Finish/Eject} forces printing on a network.

Keystroke	Keystroke macros that begin with [VISUAL EXIT] and perform
Macros		no other Visual Mode functions can now be used from Command
		Mode. For example, "[VISUAL EXIT] EXA" exits VEDIT PLUS from
		both the Command and Visual modes.

EO		The option "Reset UNDO stack when entering DOS" has been
		deleted. EO 17 to enable V-SWAP has been added.



		ADDITIONAL CHANGES FROM 3.20
		----------------------------

Point & Shoot	Files can be selected via Point & Shoot.

		The "RF" command accesses Point & Shoot from the macro
		language.

Record Macro	Keystroke macros may be added by recording keystrokes while
		you perform actual editing.

Binary Editing	VEDIT can edit files containing CTRL-Z and binary files such
		as .EXE files and database files.

Save		{CONFIG, Save into VEDIT} saves all configuration changes and
Configuration	optionally any new keystroke macros permanently into VEDIT.

Save As		{FILE, Save as} saves the file being edited under a new name
		and permits continued editing under this new name.

Lower/Upper	The previous {EDIT, Switch Lower/Upper case} function has been
Case Sub-menu	replaced with "Lower/Upper Case" sub-menu.

{GOTO,Column}	The function {GOTO, Column} jumps the cursor to any selected
		column in the current line; useful when editing very long
		lines.

[REPLACE]	After a string is found, the replacement options permit making
		the current replacement and immediately searching for the next
		occurrence.

[SEARCH]	A new search option "Word" restricts searches to complete
[REPLACE]	words, e.g. a search for "the" won't match "there".

Keyboard	{CONFIG, Misc, Keyboard repeat rate} controls the keyboard
repeat rate	repeat rate. It is only applicable to AT and PS/2 type
		keyboards. It should be set to "0" if you already have
		software which speeds up the repeat rate.

UNIX Files	{CONFIG, File handling, Newline character(s)} controls what
		type of files VEDIT is editing, in particular whether lines
		end in Carriage-return Line-feed (like DOS) or just a
		Line-feed (like UNIX).

VEDIT Home	Previously, VEDIT had to be installed in the "\VEDIT"
Directory	directory. Configuration parameter 1 now permits selecting
		any path for the "VEDIT Home Directory". Furthermore, the
		environment variable "VEDPATH" will override this setting.

Block		New configuration parameter sets an overriding (color)
Highlighting	attribute for highlighted blocks. Otherwise reverse video is
		used.

Hardware	Command Mode commands can access IBM PC hardware (peek/poke
Access		memory, I/O ports), BIOS and low level DOS functions.



		ADDITIONAL CHANGES FROM 3.10
		----------------------------

Mouse Support	Integrated mouse support added.

DOS Shell	When you shell out to DOS, VEDIT changes the DOS prompt to
		"pathname>>". This is a reminder that VEDIT is still loaded.
		Technical users can select other prompts using the "OS"
		command.

Block Search	The [SEARCH] and [REPLACE] functions can be restricted to
		operate within the highlighted block. Columnar blocks are also
		supported.

Sound		(Technical) Optional arguments to the "YA" command perform
Generation	programmable sound generation. The supplied macro
		"MARYLAMB.VDM" demonstrates this.



		ADDITIONAL CHANGES FROM 3.00
		----------------------------

Long Line	The "horizontal scroll margin" can be turned off (value 0) to
Support		disable "continuation" lines. This (default) mode permits
		using just horizontal scrolling to view very long lines.

New Block	When the cursor is within a highlighted block, [INDENT] and
Functions	[UNDENT] indent/undent the entire block.

		A highlighted block can be written to disk via {BLOCK, Write
		to disk}.
